That's it. Your Node.js application communicates with a locally running model.

No OpenAI API key, no OpenAI token billing.

The exact model identifier should match the model you pulled and its API listing.


# 4. What if your application is also running inside Docker?
This is where Docker Model Runner becomes particularly interesting.

Imagine your infrastructure:

Docker environment
Your application

Node.js / Python / NestJS

HTTP API

Docker Model Runner

Local inference engine


Qwen / Llama / Gemma

Downloaded model running on your hardware


- When your application runs inside another Docker container, it can reach Docker Desktop's Model Runner using: http://model-runner.docker.internal

For example, the OpenAI-compatible endpoint becomes: http://model-runner.docker.internal/engines/v1

You can integrate this with Docker Compose, Python, Node.js, LangChain and other frameworks.


